 <u> SUBJECT </u> :    California Peer Assistance and Review Program  
for Teachers

 <u> SOURCE </u> :     Author

 
 <u> DIGEST </u> :    This bill establishes the California Peer  
Assistance and Review Program for Teachers.

 <u> ANALYSIS </u> :    

 <u> Mentor Program </u> .  Current law establishes the California  
Mentor Teacher Program and provides that the primary  
function of mentor teachers is to provide assistance and  
                                                 CONTINUED





                                                      <u> AB 1X
 </u>                                                      Page  
2

guidance to new teachers.  Mentor teachers may also provide  
staff development for teachers and develop special  
curriculum.  The program provides funding at the rate of  
$5680 per mentor teacher, with districts allowed to  
designate up to 5% of their teachers as mentors.   
Statewide, the program currently funds approximately 14,000  
mentor teachers.

 <u> Beginning Teacher Support and Assistance (BTSA) Program </u> .   
Current law establishes the Beginning Teacher Support and  
Assistance Program (BTSA) to give guidance to first and  
second year credentialed teachers.  BTSA provides $3000 per  
beginning teacher with a local matching requirement for an  
additional $2000.  Many districts with BTSA programs use a  
portion of their Mentor program funds to meet the matching  
share requirement in BTSA.  The Governor's Budget proposes  
doubling the size of BTSA in 1999-2000 to serve all 24,000  
first and second year teachers in the state.

 <u> Teacher Intern and Pre-Intern Programs </u> .  Current law  
establishes teacher intern and pre-intern programs wherein  
individuals earn their full teaching credential while  
employed as a teacher under an emergency credential.

 <u> Instructional Time and Staff Development Reform ("Buyout"  
Program) </u> .  Current law provides funding for districts for  
three days of staff development that are outside of the  
regular instructional year.  This funding was established  
in exchange for the elimination of the authority of  
districts to have up to eight days of staff development in  
lieu of instructional time.

 <u> Teacher Evaluation </u> .  Current law requires the governing  
board of each school district to evaluate certificated  
employee performance as it reasonably relates to the  
progress of pupils toward the standards of expected pupil  
achievement established by the governing board.  These  
"Stull Act" evaluations must be completed at least once  
each year for probationary teachers and once every two  
weeks for permanent teachers.

This bill establishes the California Peer Assistance and  
Review Program for Teachers for the purpose of assisting  
veteran teachers in need of development in subject matter  







                                                      <u> AB 1X
</u>                                                      Page  
3

knowledge or teaching strategies.  Specifically, this bill:

  1. Eliminates the California Mentor Teacher Program on  
    July 1, 2001 and establishes in its place the  
    California Peer Assistance and Review Program, which  
    would become fully operational on the same date.

  2. Specifies that a school district that accepts state  
    funds for this program agrees to negotiate the  
    development and implementation of the program with the  
    exclusive representative of the certificated employees  
    in the school district, if there is an exclusive  
    representative of the certificated employees.

 3.  Specifies that a school district that elects to  
    participate in the program shall certify to the SPI  
    that it has implemented a Peer Assistance and Review  
    Program for Teachers.

 4.  Specifies that, beginning July 1, 2001, a school  
    district that does not participate in this program is  
    not eligible for funding for the following programs:

    A.    The Peer Assistance and Review Program

    B.    Specified block grant per ADA funding

    C.    The Administrator Training and Evaluation Program

    D.    The Instructional Time and Staff Development  
       Reform Program

    E.    School development plans

  5. Specifies that a district that receives funding for  
    participating in the Peer Assistance and Review Program  
    may also use the funding for the BTSA program, the  
    pre-intern program, the Mentor program activities as  
    allowed on January 1, 1999 or programs that support  
    training and development of new teachers.

  6. Requires, at a minimum, the following principles to be  
    included in locally developed peer review programs:








                                                      <u> AB 1X
 </u>                                                      Page  
4

    A.     Teacher participants in the program shall have  
       permanent status and volunteer to participate or be  
       referred for participation in the program as a  
       result of their biennial evaluation.

    B.     Performance goals for an individual teacher  
       shall be aligned with pupil learning goals.

    C.     Assistance and review shall include multiple  
       observations of a teacher during periods of  
       classroom instruction.

    D.     The program shall expect and strongly encourage  
       a cooperative relationship between the consulting  
       teacher and the principal with respect to peer  
       assistance and review.

    E.     Sufficient staff development activities shall be  
       provided to assist teachers to improve their  
       teaching skills and knowledge.

    F.     A monitoring component with a written record  
       shall be included.

    G.     The final evaluation of a teacher's  
       participation in the program shall be made available  
       for placement in the teacher's personnel file.

  7. Requires a consulting teacher participating in this  
    program to:

    A.     Be a credentialed classroom teacher with  
       permanent status.

    B.     Have substantial recent experience in classroom  
       instruction.

    C.     Have demonstrated exemplary teaching ability.

  8. Requires a joint teacher administrator peer review  
    panel to be established by participating districts to  
    govern the Peer Assistance and Review Program.  A  
    majority of the panel shall be teachers.  The panel  
    must select consulting teachers for the program, review  







                                                      <u> AB 1X
 </u>                                                      Page  
5

    peer review reports, and make recommendations to the  
    school district governing board regarding program  
    participants.

  9. Requires the joint peer review panel to annually  
    evaluate the impact of the Peer Assistance and Review  
    Program.

 10. Requires the Superintendent of Public Instruction  
    (SPI), subject to the availability of funding in the  
    annual Budget Act, to contract with an independent  
    evaluator on or before December 15, 2002, to prepare a  
    comprehensive evaluation of the implementation, impact,  
    cost, and benefit of the Peer Assistance and Review  
    Program.  This evaluation will be required to be  
    delivered to the Legislature, the Governor, and  
    interested parties on or before January 1, 2004.

 11. Requires that a certificated employee who receives an  
    unsatisfactory rating in the biennial evaluation to  
    participate in the Peer Assistance and Review (PAR)  
    Program, if the employing district participates in the  
    PAR program.

 12. Requires school district governing boards to evaluate  
    certificated employee performance on the progress of  
    pupils toward the state-adopted academic content  
    standards as measured by state-adopted criterion  
    referenced assessments.

 13. Appropriates $125, 082,000 (Proposition 98) from the  
    General Fund for the 1999-2000 fiscal year, with $41.8  
    million for Planning for the new Peer Assistance and  
    Review Program, $83.2 million for the existing Mentor  
    Teacher Program, and $82,000 for support services for  
    the Peer Assistance and Review Program for Teachers.

     Provides incentive funding of $2,800 per consulting  
    teacher for districts that implement the program by  
    July 1, 2000.  Authorizes incentives of $1,000 per  
    consulting teacher for those districts which implement  
    by July 1, 2001.

 14. Specifies that not more than 5% of the funds received  







                                                      <u> AB 1X
 </u>                                                      Page  
6

    may be expended for administrative expenses.

 15. States the intent of the Legislature that the funding  
    for 2000-2001 and beyond for the Peer Assistance and  
    Review Program shall be at least $125 million, with  
    each district's share to be the same as it received in  
    1999-2000 for the Mentor Program plus the incentive  
    funds for Peer Assistance and Review. 

 <u> Comments </u> 

 <u> Part of a Bigger Package </u> .  This bill is one of four special  
session bills sponsored by the Governor's Office to improve  
California's public school system.  The other three bills  
are:

1.AB 2X (Mazzoni), which establishes initiatives in the  
  areas of K-12 reading instruction and preparation of  
  teachers and principals.

2.SB 1X (Alpert), which establishes a school accountability  
  system

3.SB 2X (O'Connell), which establishes a high school exit  
  exam.

